[01:00.00] How did you people live out there in Oklahoma?
[01:03.00] Did you live pretty well?
[01:05.00] Well, uh...
[01:07.00] How did you place sleep?
[01:09.00] How was it?
[01:11.00] I don't know, Alan.
[01:13.00] To start with, I was a little bit different from...
[01:16.00] I wasn't in the class that John Steinbeck called the Oaks,
[01:21.00] because my dad, to start with, was worth about $35,000 or $40,000,
[01:26.00] and he had everything hunky-dory.
[01:29.00] Then he started having a little bad luck.
[01:31.00] In fact, our whole family had a little bit of it.
[01:33.00] I don't know whether it's worth talking about or not.
[01:39.00] I never do talk it much,
[01:41.00] but then on this six-room house burned down that I told you about,
[01:47.00] just a day or two after it was built.
[01:50.00] It was supposed to be one of the biggest finest in that whole country.
[01:53.00] Well, right after that,
[01:56.00] a 14-year-old sister
[02:01.00] either set herself afire or caught afire accidentally.
[02:06.00] There's two different stories got out about it.
[02:10.00] Anyway, she was having a little difficulty with her school work,
[02:14.00] and she had to stay home and do some work.
[02:17.00] She caught afire while she was doing some ironing that afternoon
[02:20.00] on the old kerosene stove.
[02:23.00] It was highly unsafe and highly uncertain in them days,
[02:26.00] and this one blew it up, caught her afire,
[02:29.00] and she run around the house about twice
[02:31.00] before anybody could catch her.
[02:33.00] The next day, she died.
[02:36.00] And my mother,
[02:38.00] that was a little bit too much for her nerves or something.
[02:47.00] I don't know exactly how it was,
[02:49.00] but my mother died in the insane asylum at Norm in Oklahoma.
[02:59.00] Then about that same time,
[03:02.00] my father mysteriously, for some reason or other,
[03:07.00] caught afire.
[03:08.00] A lot of people say that he set himself afire.
[03:10.00] Others say that he caught afire accidentally.
[03:14.00] I always will think that he'd done it on purpose
[03:16.00] because he'd lost all his money,
[03:18.00] lost his hog ranch.
[03:20.00] He used to raise some of the best
[03:22.00] Poland, China pure blood hogs in that whole country
[03:26.00] and had something proud to work for.
[03:30.00] And felt like that he was part of the world
[03:33.00] and that he was doing some good and working hard.
[03:38.00] All my brothers and sisters,
[03:40.00] I got another sister and two brothers,
[03:44.00] they all felt pretty good until
[03:48.00] all these things happened and they found their self-scattered,
[03:51.00] all those kids had scattered out
[03:53.00] and be adopted by different families.
[03:55.00] I lived with a family of people, there was 11 of us.
[03:58.00] Lived in a little two-room shack.
[04:00.00] I lived with these people several years.
[04:02.00] Her name is Sam White
[04:04.00] and his family and he still lives
[04:06.00] within about a half a block of the same old house
[04:10.00] that he lived in in them days.
[04:13.00] And in the old house with 11 of us sleeping in two rooms,
[04:18.00] we had two or three beds, you know.
[04:22.00] So we'd sleep some of us at the head
[04:25.00] and some of us at the foot
[04:27.00] and had everybody's feet and everybody's faces,
[04:30.00] you know how that is.
[04:34.00] Then after that, I don't know,
[04:36.00] I kind of took to the road.
[04:37.00] I hit the road one day,
[04:39.00] the first day that I ever hit the highway
[04:41.00] to be what's called a rambling man
[04:44.00] or a hobo or a tramp was in 1927.
[04:48.00] How old were you then?
[04:49.00] At that time I was about 17 years old.
[04:52.00] What caused you to leave on that particular day,
[04:55.00] at that time, do you remember?
[04:57.00] Well, I was adopted then by another family of people
[05:05.00] that had a little more money and a little more everything
[05:08.00] and was members of the very high
[05:10.00] and the important lodges around over town
[05:12.00] and they said it was a pity
[05:14.00] that so many of us had to live the way we did
[05:16.00] and not know where our next bite was coming from.
[05:20.00] So they said that in order to relieve me
[05:23.00] and the suffering of this family too
[05:26.00] that I was living with,
[05:27.00] that they'd take me up to their house
[05:29.00] and I could live with them
[05:31.00] so I went up and lived with them.
[05:33.00] And they had a dead gum,
[05:35.00] dead gum little old bandy hen
[05:38.00] that sat out on the ice box and roosted out there
[05:41.00] like she owned that whole part of town
[05:43.00] and my job, mainly while I was living with that family of people
